# ADR-004 — Actor

> **Any element that intentionally occupies a role within a creative representation.**

**Status:** Accepted

---

# Context

Early iterations of the Creative Representation Layer used the term
**Actor** to describe the primary elements within a creative work.

However, "Actor" proved insufficient because it implied human or
living subjects and did not naturally extend to products, vehicles,
architecture, landscapes, or other non-living elements.

Alternative terms such as **Entity**, **Subject**, **Character**, and
**Element** were considered.

While technically correct, these terms either originated from software
engineering or failed to capture the narrative nature of creative work.

VizClick models creative intent rather than physical reality.

In a creative representation:

- a car can be the protagonist
- a luxury watch can communicate prestige
- a building can project authority
- a tree can evoke loneliness
- a coffee cup can become the central focus of a story

The architecture therefore requires a concept that represents intentional
creative roles rather than real-world classifications.

---

# Decision

VizClick adopts **Actor** as the primary abstraction for every intentional
element within the Creative Representation Layer.

An Actor is not defined by what it is.

An Actor is defined by the role it plays within the creative work.

An Actor may represent:

- a person
- an animal
- a product
- a vehicle
- a building
- an object
- a landscape
- a fictional creature
- any other element capable of carrying creative meaning

Actors are renderer-independent and remain consistent regardless of the
target rendering system.

---

# Responsibilities

An Actor represents the complete creative description of a single
intentional element.

An Actor may contain:

- Concept
- Properties
- Appearance
- Performance
- Pose
- Motion
- Expression

Not every Actor requires every component.

For example:

- a human may contain Performance and Pose
- a luxury watch may contain Appearance and Expression
- a mountain may only contain Appearance

The representation remains flexible while preserving a consistent
architectural model.

---

# Relationships

Actors exist independently.

Meaning emerges through Relationships.

Relationships describe how Actors connect to one another.

Examples include:

- social relationships
- physical interactions
- spatial relationships
- visual relationships
- narrative relationships

A "couple" is therefore not an Actor.

It is two Human Actors connected by a Romantic Relationship.

Likewise:

- a family
- a sports team
- a crowd
- a wedding party

are represented through Actors and Relationships rather than standalone
concepts.

---

# Design Principles

## Creative Role

Actors represent creative roles rather than physical categories.

Their purpose is to express creative intent.

---

## Renderer Independence

Actors never contain renderer-specific syntax,
prompt engineering, or optimization rules.

Those belong exclusively to Renderer Adapters.

---

## Composability

Complex scenes emerge through combinations of multiple Actors and their
Relationships rather than specialized object types.

This keeps the architecture simple while allowing unlimited complexity.

---

## Extensibility

The Actor abstraction supports every creative domain envisioned for
VizClick, including:

- photography
- cinema
- illustration
- animation
- architecture
- product visualization
- game development
- industrial design
- future rendering systems

No architectural changes are required to support new creative domains.

---

# Consequences

## Positive

- Renderer-independent representation
- Unified abstraction across all creative media
- Eliminates special-case object types
- Simplifies scene composition
- Enables expressive relationship modeling
- Scales naturally to complex creative works

## Trade-offs

- Requires explicit Relationship modeling
- Introduces an additional abstraction compared to simple object lists

These trade-offs are accepted because they produce a more expressive,
consistent, and future-proof representation of creativity.

---

# Philosophy

Actors are not defined by what they are.

Actors are defined by the role they perform within the creative work.

Creativity assigns meaning.

The Actor carries that meaning.

---

# Guiding Principle

> Everything can become an Actor when it serves the story.